  "subtitle": "A child's 'learning computer' whose flat membrane keyboard is re-labeled by a printed mylar overlay sheet per cartridge",
  "why_scout_cares": "The Children's Discovery System (1981) is an 8-bit learning console for ages 6-11 whose interaction model is a printed, textured mylar overlay sheet: each cartridge comes with its own printed surface that the child lays over the flat membrane keyboard, physically re-typing what every key means without changing the hardware. It is a print-on-membrane, context-sensitive keyboard — a static physical 'skin' years before context-sensitive on-screen keys. Distinct from every keyboard exhibit in the collection: not a mechanical re-mapped keyboard (the Bat Keyboard's keycaps), not a software relabeled one (Datapoint screens), but a paper label that literally re-defines the input surface per program. A clean, well-documented, image-available early-80s children's computer.",
